<body>
<div class="page-wrap">

<div class="header-bar">
  <div class="header-top">
    <span class="brand-name">Genovean</span>
    <div class="header-nav">
      <span class="course-crumb">Seeing It Clearly</span>
      <span class="crumb-sep">/</span>
      <span class="course-crumb">Module 2</span>
      <span class="crumb-sep">/</span>
      <span class="crumb-active">Section 2 of 3</span>
    </div>
    <span class="section-tag">Phase 1 &middot; Awareness</span>
  </div>
  <h1 class="tool-title">Signal Spotter: What It Means</h1>
  <p class="tool-subtitle">Having a pattern is one thing. Understanding what that pattern is likely to indicate is another. This section helps you interpret what you have documented, calmly, clearly, and without jumping to conclusions.</p>
</div>

<div class="progress-wrap no-print">
  <span id="prog-text" style="white-space:nowrap;font-size:12px;color:#8A8A84;">0 of 3 signals reviewed</span>
  <div class="progress-track"><div class="progress-fill" id="prog-fill"></div></div>
  <span class="progress-label" id="prog-pct">0%</span>
</div>

<div class="content-area">

<div class="section-band">
  <span class="section-icon">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 10 10" fill="none">
      <path d="M2 8L5 2l3 6" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
      <path d="M3.2 6.2h3.6" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="1" stroke-linecap="round"/>
    </svg>
  </span>
  What It Means
</div>

<!-- SIGNAL 1 -->
<div class="signal-card" id="card-1">
  <div class="signal-header" onclick="toggleCard(1)">
    <span class="signal-num">1</span>
    <span class="signal-label">Normal aging or something more -- you can tell the difference</span>
    <span class="signal-status" id="status-1">Not reviewed</span>
    <svg class="chevron" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
  </div>
  <div class="detail-body">
    <p class="signal-desc">There is a meaningful difference between changes that are expected as a normal part of aging and changes that suggest something worth paying close attention to. Normal aging tends to be gradual, consistent, and proportional -- things take a little longer, energy is slightly lower, some names and words take a moment longer to find. What you are looking for in this course is something different: changes that are disproportionate to what aging alone would explain, that appear suddenly or accelerate quickly, that cluster with other changes, or that represent a significant departure from the person's established baseline. You do not need a medical degree to tell the difference. You need honesty about what you have actually been seeing.</p>
    <div class="guidance-box">
      <p class="guidance-label">What distinguishes normal aging from something more</p>
      <p class="guidance-text">Normal aging: moving a little slower, needing reading glasses, occasionally forgetting a name but remembering it later, taking longer to learn new technology, preferring familiar routines. Something more: getting lost in familiar places, forgetting whole conversations that happened recently, significant changes in personality or behaviour, inability to manage tasks that were routine for decades, physical decline that is faster than gradual. The key question is always: is this proportionate to age alone, or is there something else driving it?</p>
    </div>
    <div class="observe-box">
      <p class="observe-label">How to apply this to what you have seen</p>
      <p class="observe-text">Go back to your notes from Modules 1 and 2. For each thing you recorded, ask yourself honestly: could this be explained by normal aging alone? If the answer is yes, note that. If the answer is no -- if what you have seen feels disproportionate, clustered, or clearly new -- that is your signal. You are not diagnosing anything. You are applying a simple and honest filter to what you have already observed.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="freq-label">How would you describe this signal in relation to your parent?</p>
    <div class="freq-row">
      <button class="freq-btn" onclick="setFreq(1,'regular',this)">Happening regularly</button>
      <button class="freq-btn" onclick="setFreq(1,'yes',this)">Yes, I have seen this</button>
      <button class="freq-btn" onclick="setFreq(1,'sometimes',this)">Once or twice</button>
      <button class="freq-btn" onclick="setFreq(1,'no',this)">Not yet</button>
    </div>
    <p class="notes-label">Your notes</p>
    <div class="notes-example">
      <p class="notes-example-label">Example of a useful note</p>
      <p class="notes-example-text">"When I apply the normal aging filter to what I have been seeing, most of it does not fit. Forgetting a name occasionally, yes, that is normal. Not being able to manage bills that she handled without a second thought for forty years -- that is not normal aging. Getting lost on a road she has driven weekly for two decades -- that is not normal aging. The things I have documented are not what normal aging looks like."</p>
    </div>
    <textarea class="notes-field" placeholder="Apply the normal aging filter to your observations. Write which things could be explained by age alone and which cannot. Be specific about why." oninput="updateSummary()"></textarea>
    <div class="notes-why">
      <strong>Why this note matters.</strong> This is often the note that changes everything. Families spend months or years accepting things as normal aging that are not. Writing it down clearly -- this is not what normal aging looks like -- is a significant moment. It deserves to be recorded precisely.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SIGNAL 2 -->
<div class="signal-card" id="card-2">
  <div class="signal-header" onclick="toggleCard(2)">
    <span class="signal-num">2</span>
    <span class="signal-label">Multiple things shifting at the same time points to something underneath</span>
    <span class="signal-status" id="status-2">Not reviewed</span>
    <svg class="chevron" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
  </div>
  <div class="detail-body">
    <p class="signal-desc">When several things change around the same time, the home, the food, the going out, the mood, the memory, the most important question to ask is not "what is wrong with each of these?" but "what could be driving all of them?" The most common answer is that an underlying condition -- cognitive, physical, emotional, or some combination -- has reached a point where it is showing up in multiple areas of daily life simultaneously. The individual signals are the visible surface. What you are looking for in this signal is the shape of what is underneath them. You do not need to name it. You need to see that it is there.</p>
    <div class="guidance-box">
      <p class="guidance-label">What clustering tells you</p>
      <p class="guidance-text">When change appears in one area, it could be circumstantial. When it appears across three or four areas at the same time, circumstance is no longer a sufficient explanation. The home, the food, the social withdrawal, the financial management -- each of these draws on different skills and different parts of daily life. When all of them shift together, something central has changed. That is the most important thing this signal is designed to help you see.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="observe-box">
      <p class="observe-label">How to apply this to what you have seen</p>
      <p class="observe-text">Look at the areas from Module 1 where you marked something. Write them in a list. Then ask: did these start shifting around the same period? Could a single underlying cause explain more than one of them? You are not trying to name a diagnosis. You are trying to see whether your observations share a common root -- because if they do, that is the most useful insight you will take away from this module.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="freq-label">How would you describe this signal in relation to your parent?</p>
    <div class="freq-row">
      <button class="freq-btn" onclick="setFreq(2,'regular',this)">Happening regularly</button>
      <button class="freq-btn" onclick="setFreq(2,'yes',this)">Yes, I have seen this</button>
      <button class="freq-btn" onclick="setFreq(2,'sometimes',this)">Once or twice</button>
      <button class="freq-btn" onclick="setFreq(2,'no',this)">Not yet</button>
    </div>
    <p class="notes-label">Your notes</p>
    <div class="notes-example">
      <p class="notes-example-label">Example of a useful note</p>
      <p class="notes-example-text">"The home slipping, the eating changing, the going out reducing, the mood flattening, all of these started becoming noticeable around the same period, roughly six to eight months ago. I think they share a root. My sense is that something in his cognitive ability to plan, manage, and motivate himself has reduced. Each individual thing I noticed is a symptom of that. This is not five separate problems. It is one problem showing up in five places."</p>
    </div>
    <textarea class="notes-field" placeholder="List the areas where you have noticed change. Do they cluster in time? Could they share a common root? What do you think is underneath them?" oninput="updateSummary()"></textarea>
    <div class="notes-why">
      <strong>Why this note matters.</strong> Seeing the pattern underneath the individual signals is the most powerful shift in perspective this course can produce. Once you have written it clearly, you are no longer managing a series of unrelated problems. You are dealing with one situation that requires one honest, integrated response.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SIGNAL 3 -->
<div class="signal-card" id="card-3">
  <div class="signal-header" onclick="toggleCard(3)">
    <span class="signal-num">3</span>
    <span class="signal-label">Some signals carry more weight than others, and you can identify which</span>
    <span class="signal-status" id="status-3">Not reviewed</span>
    <svg class="chevron" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"><path d="M4 6l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
  </div>
  <div class="detail-body">
    <p class="signal-desc">Not all signals are equal in what they indicate or in how urgently they need to be responded to. A home that is less tidy than it used to be is worth noting. An older adult who is not managing their medication correctly is worth acting on. Driving concerns that involve safety -- actual near misses, getting lost while driving, new vehicle damage that cannot be explained -- carry immediate risk implications. Cognitive signals that are both consistent and clearly different from baseline suggest something that should involve a GP sooner rather than later. This signal asks you to look across everything you have documented and identify which two or three observations are the ones that carry the most weight -- the ones you would bring to someone else if you had to explain why you are concerned.</p>
    <div class="guidance-box">
      <p class="guidance-label">What gives a signal more weight</p>
      <p class="guidance-text">Safety implications, anything that creates immediate physical risk to your parent or others. Consistency, something that happens repeatedly, not just once. Severity of departure from baseline -- the more it differs from who they have always been, the more weight it carries. Medical relevance -- signals around medication, cognition, significant weight loss, or mobility all have direct health implications. A signal that ticks two or more of these criteria is one you should take seriously now, not later.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="observe-box">
      <p class="observe-label">How to apply this to what you have seen</p>
      <p class="observe-text">Read back through your notes from this module and Module 1. Identify the two or three observations that feel most significant, the ones you find yourself thinking about most, the ones that have made you feel most concerned. Write them out and explain briefly why each one carries more weight than the others. You are not dismissing everything else. You are identifying where your attention needs to be focused first.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="freq-label">How would you describe this signal in relation to your parent?</p>
    <div class="freq-row">
      <button class="freq-btn" onclick="setFreq(3,'regular',this)">Happening regularly</button>
      <button class="freq-btn" onclick="setFreq(3,'yes',this)">Yes, I have seen this</button>
      <button class="freq-btn" onclick="setFreq(3,'sometimes',this)">Once or twice</button>
      <button class="freq-btn" onclick="setFreq(3,'no',this)">Not yet</button>
    </div>
    <p class="notes-label">Your notes</p>
    <div class="notes-example">
      <p class="notes-example-label">Example of a useful note</p>
      <p class="notes-example-text">"The two things that carry the most weight for me are the driving and the medication. The driving because there is a real safety risk to her and to others and because it has happened more than once on familiar roads. The medication because she has multiple conditions that require consistent management and I no longer have confidence that this is happening. The home and the food are serious but they are not urgent in the same way. These two are."</p>
    </div>
    <textarea class="notes-field" placeholder="Identify the two or three signals that carry the most weight. Write each one and explain why it is more significant than the others, safety, consistency, medical relevance, or departure from baseline." oninput="updateSummary()"></textarea>
    <div class="notes-why">
      <strong>Why this note matters.</strong> This is the note you will return to most often. When you are trying to explain your concern to a sibling, a doctor, or your parent themselves, you will not lead with everything. You will lead with the two or three things that carry the most weight. Writing them clearly now means you are ready for that conversation when it comes.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

